  <details class="more">
    <summary><span class="chev"></span><span class="label-closed">Read the full description</span><span class="label-open">Hide description</span></summary>
    <div class="more-body">
      <p>On near term quantum hardware the binding constraint is not the model, it is the qubit count: you can only encode as many features as you have qubits. This paper asks whether classical compression can buy quantum capacity. We reduce 30 clinical features to 5 principal components, encode each component as a rotation angle on one qubit, and let a variational circuit with ring entanglement supply the nonlinear mapping before a small classical head makes the call.</p>
      <p>On the Wisconsin Diagnostic Breast Cancer set, the five qubit hybrid with PCA reached 96.49% accuracy with recall 0.98 and F1 0.97, slightly ahead of the 30 feature classical baseline at 95.61% and with fewer missed malignancies. The ten qubit circuit without PCA managed only 88.60%, which is the result I find most instructive: adding qubits without fixing the encoding made the model worse.</p>
      <p>The robustness test is the other half of the story. Under additive Gaussian noise at 0.5 of each feature's standard deviation, the hybrid held 63.16% accuracy while the matched classical model collapsed to 18.42%. Circuits were simulated in PennyLane with a PyTorch head, trained end to end with parameter shift gradients.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
